Everything under casita/synthgen/ is derived from FetalSynGen

    https://github.com/Medical-Image-Analysis-Laboratory/fetalsyngen

which is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. A copy of that
licence is included at licenses/Apache-2.0.txt. Those files remain under
Apache-2.0; the rest of this repository is MIT.

Vendored at commit 88442ce74b8541ba9b1b4c718699650b644b7c7c:

    casita/synthgen/data/datasets.py
    casita/synthgen/generator/model.py
    casita/synthgen/generator/intensity/rand_gmm.py
    casita/synthgen/generator/deformation/affine_nonrigid.py
    casita/synthgen/generator/augmentation/synthseg.py
    casita/synthgen/generator/augmentation/artifacts.py
    casita/synthgen/generator/artifacts/simulate_reco.py
    casita/synthgen/generator/artifacts/utils.py
    casita/synthgen/utils/image_reading.py
    casita/synthgen/utils/generation.py

Modifications made to those files:

  * import paths rewritten from `fetalsyngen.*` to `casita.synthgen.*`
  * `pd.read_csv(label_map_csv)` reads with `comment="#"` so the documented
    label-map template parses

No other changes. The SVoRT subpackage and the slice-acquisition CUDA
extension are not included: they are reachable only from super-resolution
artifact simulation, which CASITA does not use.

FetalSynGen is itself based on SynthSeg, Brain-ID and SVoRT; see the upstream
repository for its own attributions.
